  "source_method_observations": [
    {"physical_page":529,"printed_page":463,"classification":"reported modeling and analytical method","observation":"Capacities based on AISC LRFD 2005 provisions; resistance factors set to 1.0 to obtain failure loads rather than design loads. Floor slab contact transfers gravity without lateral shear to girders; break elements represent temperature-dependent shear-stud failure. These statements are not reported specimen-test results."},
    {"physical_page":530,"printed_page":464,"classification":"reported analytical method","observation":"Weld equations use E70xx electrode classification and effective weld area; eccentric weld-group loading uses the instantaneous-center-of-rotation method."},
    {"physical_page":531,"printed_page":465,"classification":"reported assumption and analytical adjustment","observation":"Threads assumed excluded from shear plane, called conservative in source. NIST justifies dividing nominal bolt shear stresses by 0.8 on an asserted even bolt-load distribution in shear connections, contrasting splice connections. A325 becomes 75 ksi from 60 ksi; A490 becomes 93.75 ksi from 75 ksi. The engineering adequacy of these assumptions is not established by reproducing the division."},
    {"physical_page":532,"printed_page":466,"classification":"reported analytical method","observation":"Tear-out evaluated for beam web and connection plate. Block-shear capacity selected from the smallest evaluated failure-path result."},
    {"physical_page":533,"printed_page":467,"classification":"reported analytical assumption","observation":"Uniform tension stress assumed with Ubs=1. Minimum yield or rupture shear contribution used. Figures distinguish shear plate and web failures, and horizontal-force failure paths."},
    {"physical_page":534,"printed_page":468,"classification":"reported source-dependent prediction","observation":"Stud strength depends on geometry, concrete, location and stud strength. Rambo-Roddenberry 2002 is cited for a prediction with loading perpendicular to metal-deck ribs. Rp=0.68 is conditional on e>=2.2 in and strong position; Rn=1.0 for one stud per rib or staggered position; Rd=1.0 for strong-position studs. The claim that Fu=65 ksi predicts published experiments is an attribution; this audit has not independently reviewed those experiments."},
    {"physical_page":535,"printed_page":469,"classification":"reported averaging and transferred model choice","observation":"AISC values 21.5 kip strong and 17.2 kip weak are averaged because actual stud side could vary; printed average is 19.4 kip. Chosen capacity is 19.5 kip for forces both perpendicular and parallel to ribs. This averaging and directional transfer are not demonstrated in these pages by WTC7-specific tests. Arc-spot welds to girder top flanges were neglected based on an approximately 2 kip calculation and asserted larger thermal restraint force. Shear-connection failure loads here use room-temperature material properties. STC/STP at Columns79/81 evaluated for horizontal loads only because vertical seat capacity was reported much larger than full-design vertical loads."},
    {"physical_page":536,"printed_page":470,"classification":"reported ratio interpretation","observation":"Tabulated vertical failure-load ratios omit load and resistance factors. No horizontal capacity/design-load ratio supplied because these shear connections were not designed for horizontal loads; different load directions can change failure modes. Ratios are not failure probabilities or progressive-collapse resistance factors."}
  ]
